      "rev_n_1_9743fb_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_9743fb_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_9743fb",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible values of a, b, and c within the range [1, N] and check if they satisfy the conditions a+b, b+c, and c+a being multiples of K.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500134.774257
      },
      "rev_n_2_bd4392_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_bd4392_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_bd4392",
        "thought_text": "Use modular arithmetic properties to simplify the problem. Since a+b, b+c, and c+a must be multiples of K, we can reduce the problem to finding solutions where a ≡ -b (mod K), b ≡ -c (mod K), and c ≡ -a (mod K). This reduces the problem to counting valid combinations of residues modulo K.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500134.774274
      },
      "rev_n_3_d4e748_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_d4e748_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_d4e748",
        "thought_text": "Precompute the count of each residue modulo K up to N. Then, use combinatorial mathematics to calculate the number of valid triples based on these counts. Specifically, for each residue r, calculate the number of ways to choose a, b, and c such that their sums are multiples of K.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500134.774285
      },
      "rev_n_1_9743fb_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_9743fb_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_9743fb",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible values of a and b within the range [1, N], and calculate c as c = (K - (a + b) % K) % K. This ensures that a+b, b+c, and c+a are all multiples of K. Check if c is within the range [1, N]. If it is, increment the count.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_9743fb_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_07c3d3a7a9",
        "created_at": 1794500148.9137225
      },
      "rev_n_2_bd4392_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_bd4392_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_bd4392",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to efficiently count the number of valid triples (a, b, c) by keeping track of the counts of residues modulo K for each position in the triple. This approach reduces the time complexity significantly compared to the brute-force method.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_bd4392_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_7aa5734d04",
        "created_at": 1794500172.1745102
      },
      "rev_n_3_d4e748_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_d4e748_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_d4e748",
        "thought_text": "Revised Thought-1: Instead of directly counting combinations based on residues, consider the distribution of numbers within each residue class modulo K. For each residue class, determine how many numbers fall into it. Use this information to compute the number of valid triples by considering all possible pairs of residues that sum to another residue modulo K. This approach ensures that we correctly account for the constraints and avoid over-counting or missing valid triples.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_d4e748_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_98b4ed8043",
